derivations.claim:
By the construction of the normalization, $B$ is a finite $A$-module, so $
u$ is finite; and $k[X^{
u}]=B$ has fraction field $k(X^{
u})=\operatorname{Frac}(B)=\operatorname{Frac}(A)=k(X)$, so the pullback $
u^*$ is an isomorphism of function fields. Thus $
u$ is finite and birational [F1, F4].

Chartwise normalization. By [F1] each chart $U_i$ has its affine normalization $
u_i\colon U_i^{
u}	o U_i$, finite, surjective and birational, with $U_i^{
u}$ normal and $k[U_i^{
u}]=B_i\subseteq k(X)$.

Gluing. Since principal opens cover each overlap and the identifications of step 2.1 are compatible, the varieties $U_i^{
u}$, together with their maps to $X$, glue along the open overlaps by the standard gluing of compatible classical charts; the maps $
u_i$ agree on overlaps because they are determined by the inclusions $A_i\hookrightarrow B_i$ in the common field [F3, F4], so they glue to a morphism $
u\colon X^{
u}	o X$. The glued variety $X^{
u}$ is normal, since normality is local and each chart is normal [F1]; it is separated because $
u$ is finite, the diagonal over $X$ being affine-locally cut out by the surjection $B_i\otimes_{A_i}B_i	o B_i$; and the morphism $
u$ is finite, surjective and birational because these properties are affine-local on the target and hold on every chart by [F1] and [F2].

Gluing and uniqueness. On an overlap $Y_i\cap Y_j$, the morphisms $g_i$ and $g_j$ have the same pullback on the common function field $k(X)$, because both are determined by the identifications $k[U_i^{
u}]=B_i\subseteq k(X)=k(Y)$; hence they agree on the dense open intersection by [F4], and they glue to a morphism $g\colon Y	o X^{
u}$ over $X$ with $
u\circ g=f$. If $g'$ is another such morphism, then $g$ and $g'$ have the same pullback on rational functions on $X^{
u}$ (both equal the given identification $k(X^{
u})=k(X)	o k(Y)$), so they agree on a dense open and hence everywhere by the equality lemma [F4]; this proves uniqueness. The affine statement of step 1.1 is the chartwise content of the construction, and the morphism $X^{
u}	o X$ is the normalization by [F1].
